    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\n6 New Hot Wheels To Think of for Your Collection\n27 photos\nPhoto: Mattel\nI just started working on my final scale model car unboxing story for October. After going for Matchbox and Tarmac Works in the first two ones, I figured I’d move on to Hot Wheels. I found 10 items that are over 20 years old, and opening them felt like using a time machine.\nRight now I’m almost all set for both November and December, with over 40 tiny cars to go through. I’ve got Mini GT, Kaido House, IXO, Maisto, Matchbox and Hot Wheels on my list. And I know that for 2027 I want to start looking at brands like Greenlight, BBR, and Autoworld.\nJust as Mattel comes up with so many new models each year, I’m sure there’s plenty to explore over at those other manufacturers as well. Meanwhile, I’ve just seen the first few photos of the upcoming Porsche 911 Carrera T Super Treasure Hunt with wheels on.\nThe last time I talked about it, it was just the shell, and it didn’t feel nearly as exciting as it does now. Seeing these 10-spoke Real Riders makes me forget what I said about the livery, and I can see how many collectors will be doing their best to either find this car in the wild or source it from eBay. You’ll have to wait several months before that, though.\nIf you need a quick fix, I’ve got something for you towards the end of this story. Let’s focus on the next Fast and Furious set first. I don’t know about you but I’ve lost all faith in this theme. Mattel is just taking things too far, and you can easily tell how most enthusiasts feel about it by simply reading the online comments.\nPhoto: Hot Wheels Malaysia I understand the commercial reasoning behind the plan, but it seems like it’s all downhill from here. We had four Silver Series waves in 2026: Tokyo Drift, Dream Lineup, Race-Off and Silver Anniversary. That’s a total of 20 cars: do you want to guess how many of them I bought? The answer is 0.\nNext year is already off to a strange start with “Nitro Grid” looking like Fast and Furious collided with Neon Speeders . “Fast Life” is just as confusing, and now it seems that the third of four potential waves will shift the theme into “Boost Mode”.\nThat’s an odd choice considering at least two of the vehicles in here are running on naturally-aspirated engines. You could just go with the flow and ignore that information considering the liveries aren’t accurate either. The '11 Dodge Charger R/T is #1 of 5 for the set, and I had forgotten Brendon Vetuskey designed this.\nIt debuted in 2011 and already rose to fame the following year by joining the Super Treasure Hunt series. Its first Fast and Furious iteration arrived in 2013. It went through a Batman phase between 2020 and 2025, with just one Color Shifters release in 2023. I guess you could be happy about seeing it on a new set of Silver Series wheels.\nPhoto: Hot Wheels Malaysia Or you could ask where Han’s Veilside RX-7 is after all these years of waiting for it. Leon’s Nissan Skyline is next in line, and what used to be a cool early 2000s livery is currently just barely recognizable. I’m being particularly nitpicky about this one as it was one of my favorite cars in the first movie.\nThis will be its fifth time paying tribute to that vehicle, whereas we’ve only had one variation for the Tokyo Drift R33 (back in 2020). The Datsun 240Z was part of Fast X, and it first showed up in the 2023 Fast & Furious (Series 3) set where it was easily eclipsed by Neela’s Mazda RX-8.\nIt abandoned the basic 10-spoke wheels for a set of newer MM5 ones in 2025, and that was almost seemed like the last of it. Look at it and mark my words: it has the potential to be this set’s unofficial peg-warmer. The Nissan Silvia S13 is the only item in the mix that I might consider buying, even though I already have one at home.\nThe one I’m talking about was part of last year’s Fast & Furious 5-Pack, which uses a set of basic PR5 rims. The Star 5-Spoke design is quite a bit nicer by comparison. And seeing that I don’t even remember seeing this car in any of the movies, I don’t mind the change of livery. I might even agree with the new look on Mia’s Integra, if only for the Fuchs-inspired wheels.\nPhoto: Mattel There’s a good chance this will be the “Chase” item for the set, as it has only ever had a metal base so far. Anyway, this whole set will be cheaper then Mattel’s upcoming “special trick”, the Hot Wheels R/C 1:64 Subaru Impreza Rally (complete with a 555 number plate and working lights). I haven’t played with any of the company’s remote-controlled vehicles before, and I think it’s just too small to have some serious fun with it.\nIt’s unlikely it could perform in your backyard, and I wouldn’t like to just try using it on my desktop. I’ve heard some pretty rough stories about the Lamborghini Temerario RC previously, to the point where it might be more than just rumors. Don’t mind me though, you can try to get yours on October 23 , 2026 at 9 AM PT, if you’re OK with paying $55 (before taxes) for it.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHot Wheels Nissan THE FAST AND THE FURIOUS acura integra Dodge Subaru Impreza RC Super Treasure Hunt\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT Who Owns the World's Most Expensive Car, the Mercedes-Benz 300 SLR Uhlenhaut Coupe After All?",
